WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.078
Firmware integrity begins before the
application runs and continues through every

2
00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:04.511
update.

3
00:00:04.591 --> 00:00:07.816
Teams should define what code is trusted
at boot, what metadata is required,

4
00:00:07.896 --> 00:00:10.214
how firmware authenticity and integrity
are checked,

5
00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:12.007
and what happens when validation fails.

6
00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:14.304
These decisions should be written as
requirements,

7
00:00:14.384 --> 00:00:16.803
not left as assumptions inside
implementation details.

8
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.949
Update safety is more than a cryptography
topic.

9
00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:22.305
It is also state management, reliability,
compatibility, release control,

10
00:00:22.385 --> 00:00:23.544
and operational planning.

11
00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:26.093
A device may lose power, receive an
incompatible package,

12
00:00:26.173 --> 00:00:29.398
encounter storage problems, or need to
recover from an interrupted update.

13
00:00:29.478 --> 00:00:32.653
The update design should define safe
behavior for those cases before field

14
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:33.287
deployment.

15
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.081
Rollback policy should be intentional.

16
00:00:35.161 --> 00:00:37.580
Recovery may require returning to a
known-good version,

17
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.238
while some products also need to avoid
accepting older firmware that no longer meets

18
00:00:41.318 --> 00:00:42.376
security requirements.

19
00:00:42.456 --> 00:00:45.883
The policy should match product risk,
service needs, and support expectations,

20
00:00:45.963 --> 00:00:48.633
and it should be reviewable by engineering
and security teams.

21
00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:50.628
Update logic should avoid ambiguous
states.

22
00:00:50.708 --> 00:00:53.379
The device should know whether it is
running the current image,

23
00:00:53.459 --> 00:00:56.332
staging an update, validating an image,
recovering from a failure,

24
00:00:56.412 --> 00:00:57.419
or reporting a version.

25
00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:00.976
Support teams need enough evidence to
understand what happened without exposing

26
00:01:01.056 --> 00:01:02.619
sensitive implementation details.

27
00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:03.858
Release evidence matters.

28
00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:07.364
Artifacts should be traceable to source,
configuration, toolchain, approval,

29
00:01:07.444 --> 00:01:09.460
signing material handling, and release
notes.

30
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.715
Secure firmware update design depends on
knowing not only that an image was

31
00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:16.020
accepted, but also where it came from and
why it was approved for deployment.
